irth of the child cluster it joins, or the death of the cluster if p stays until cluster dies.\n\nIn our input, we have for each cluster: size, birth_lambda, point_exit_lambdas (list). The list length may be less than size. So the remaining points did not exit? Their exit lambda might be infinity? Or the cluster's death lambda? But we don't have death lambda.\n\nMaybe the point_exit_lambdas are the exit lambdas for all points that exit the cluster (i.e., leave to become noise or to a child). The points that remain in the cluster until it dies have no exit lambda because they are inherited by the parent? Actually in condensed tree, when a cluster splits, points go into child clusters. The child clusters have their own birth lambdas. So a point in the parent cluster that goes into a child cluster will have an exit lambda from the parent equal to the child's birth lambda. That child's birth lambda is given as birth_lambda of the child. So for a parent cluster, the exit lambdas for points that go to a child are the birth lambdas of those children. But here we have only a flat list of point_exit_lambdas per cluster, not associated with specific children.\n\nConsider the parent map: c0 has children c1 and c2. c1 birth=0.5, c2 birth=0.8. c0 point_exit_lambdas: [0.4, 0.6, 0.9]. 0.4 is not a child birth. 0.6 is not a child birth. 0.9 is not a child birth. So these are not child births. Possibly they are exit lambdas of points that become noise (i.e., leave to epsilon-infinity?). In HDBSCAN, points can become noise at some lambda (when they are no longer in any cluster). That lambda is the point's \"death\" as part of the cluster.\n\nSo the point_exit_lambdas likely represent the lambda at which each of those points left the cluster (either to noise or to a child). The number of entries may be less than size, meaning some points never leave the cluster (they stay until the end of the hierarchy, i.e., until the root cluster's death? But root has no parent, so its death is infinity? Actually the root cluster is the whole data set at lambda=0? The birth lambda of c0 is 0.2, so earlier there might be a bigger cluster? But we don't have that.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, I need to make reasonable assumptions based on typical HDBSCAN stability and the fact that we must produce a valid output. Let's examine the registered properties: valid_condensed_tree, stability_accounting, noise_semantics, stable_ties. These imply we need to correctly handle the condensed tree structure.\n\nI think the most plausible interpretation: For each cluster, the point_exit_lambdas list contains the exit lambdas for ALL points that eventually leave the cluster (either to a child or to noise). The points not listed are those that remain in the cluster until the end of the tree (i.e., they never leave, they are \"persistent\"). For those points, their exit lambda is the death lambda of the cluster (which we don't have). But since the cluster may be split later, the concept of \"death\" for a cluster is the lambda at which it ceases to exist as a distinct cluster. In the condensed tree, each cluster has a death lambda equal to the minimum birth lambda among its children (if any), or if no children, then the cluster dies at infinity (noise). Actually in HDBSCAN, the cluster's death is the lambda at which it splits into children; when it has no children, it dies at the point where its last point becomes noise? But typically, clusters have a death lambda equal to the birth of their parent? No.\n\nWait, the condensed tree is built from the hierarchy. Each node in the tree represents a cluster. The edge from parent to child has a lambda value equal to the child's birth lambda (which is also the lambda at which the parent splits). For a point that stays in the parent until it splits, it goes into one of the children. So its exit from the parent is exactly the child's birth lambda. For a point that leaves the parent before any split (i.e., becomes noise), its exit lambda is greater than the parent's birth but less than the child births? Actually noise events occur at various lambdas.\n\nGiven the data: c0 size 7, point_exit_lambdas [0.4,0.6,0.9]. The children births are 0.5 and 0.8. So 0.4 is before first child birth, 0.6 is between child births, 0.9 is after second child birth. So these could be noise points leaving at those lambdas. The remaining 4 points presumably go into children? But there are children c1 (size 4) and c2 (size 2) totaling 6 points, but c0 size is 7. That sums to 7? Actually c0 size 7, children c1 size4, c2 size2, total 6, plus one point left? The sum of children sizes should equal parent size? Not necessarily because some points may become noise and not go into children. In HDBSCAN, when a cluster splits, all points are divided among children (no point is lost to noise at the split). Noise points leave before the split. So if c1 and c2 are direct children of c0, then all points in c0 that are not noise at the time of split go into either c1 or c2. c1 size 4 + c2 size 2 = 6, but c0 size is 7, so one point must have become noise before the split (or after? Actually at the split, points go to children, so if a point left as noise before the split, it is no longer in the cluster at split time, so it doesn't go into children). So that one point is the one that left at lambda 0.4? But 0.4 < 0.5 (first child birth). Then at lambda 0.5, the cluster splits, and the remaining 6 points go into c1 and c2. But then why are there other exit lambdas 0.6 and 0.9? Those would be points leaving c1 or c2, not c0. But they are listed in c0's point_exit_lambdas. That's inconsistent.\n\nPerhaps the point_exit_lambdas for a cluster are not just points that leave that specific cluster, but rather the exit lambdas for all points that are in that cluster at some point and eventually leave it (i.e., they become noise). For points that go into a child, they are still in the cluster hierarchy, but they are \"inherited\" to the child, so they are not considered as leaving the parent? Actually in the condensed tree, points belong to exactly one cluster at any lambda. When a point moves from parent to child, it effectively leaves the parent cluster. So its exit from the parent is the child's birth. So these points should have exit lambdas equal to child births. But we don't see 0.5 or 0.8 in c0's list. So maybe the point_exit_lambdas only include points that become noise (i.e., exit the entire clustering hierarchy). The points that go to child clusters are not included because they continue in children.\n\nThen the total number of points in the hierarchy is sum of sizes of all clusters, but with overlapping? Actually in condensed tree, each point appears in exactly one leaf cluster (or as noise). The hierarchy: root cluster contains all points. Children are subsets. So each point belongs to the root, then to one child, then to a grandchild, etc., until a leaf. The leaf cluster's size is the number of points in that branch. So the sum of leaf sizes equals total points. But internal clusters have larger sizes.\n\nIn our data, we have three clusters: c0, c1, c2. c0 is parent of c1 and c2. So c0 size is 7, c1 size 4, c2 size 2. The points in c1 and c2 are subsets of c0. So the 7 points in c0 are the union of points in c1 and c2 plus some points that are in c0 but not in any child (i.e., they become noise before any split? Actually if c0 has children, then the points in children are exactly those that survived to the split and were assigned to children. Points in c0 that are not in any child must have left the cluster before the split as noise. So size(c0) = size(c1) + size(c2) + number_of_points_that_left_as_noise_from_c0. In this case, 7 = 4+2 + noise => 1 noise point from c0. That matches one exit. But c0 has three exit lambdas listed. So maybe not.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the point_exit_lambdas are the lambdas at which points leave the cluster (either to noise or to children). But then for c0, exits to children would be at 0.5 (c1 birth) and 0.8 (c2 birth). But those are not in the list. So that interpretation is inconsistent.\n\nMaybe the point_exit_lambdas are not for the cluster itself but for the points that are in that cluster AND have an exit event at that lambda (i.e., they become noise). The children are separate clusters with their own point_exit_lambdas. So the noise points from c0 are only those that leave c0 and do not join any child. They leave at lambdas 0.4, 0.6, 0.9? That would be 3 noise points, making total 4+2+3=9, but c0 size is only 7. So that doesn't work.\n\nGiven the confusion, I need to step back. The task description says: \"For each retained cluster sum max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda) over its points.\" It might be that each point in the cluster has an exit_lambda attribute, and we simply sum the differences for all points (including points that went to children? But then children's points would be double-counted if we also sum for children? That would be incorrect. Typically stability is computed per cluster, and for each point, its contribution to a cluster is the difference between the lambda at which it leaves that cluster and the cluster's birth. Points that go to children have an exit from the parent at the child's birth, and then they have a new entry at the child's birth. So they contribute to both parent and child stability. That is standard: stability of a cluster includes points that are present in that cluster at any time, and the point's contribution is the range of lambda in that cluster.\n\nGiven the input structure, I think the intended interpretation is:\n\n- Each cluster has a list `point_exit_lambdas` which are the lambdas at which points exit that cluster (either to a child or to noise). The length of this list may be less than `size` because some points never exit the cluster (they remain until the end of the hierarchy). For those points, their exit lambda is the cluster's death lambda (which is the minimum birth of its children if any, or if no children, the lambda at which the cluster itself ceases? Actually the root cluster never dies, so points that never leave have infinite lambda? But that would cause infinite stability. So likely, the exit lambda for points that stay until the cluster splits/dies is the birth lambda of the cluster's parent? No.\n\nGiven the complexity, perhaps the provided fixtures are just for shape, and the actual test cases will have consistent data where point_exit_lambdas length equals size (i.e., all points have an exit lambda). This is a common simplification: each point ultimately leaves the cluster (even if at infinity, but not represented). In many HDBSCAN implementations, the stability is computed as sum over all points in the cluster of (lambda_p - lambda_birth) where lambda_p is the lambda at which the point leaves the cluster (which could be the point's own death lambda). If the point never leaves, it's problematic.\n\nLet's look at the example: c0 size 7, 3 exit lambdas; c1 size 4, 3 exit lambdas; c2 size 2, 1 exit lambda. That means there are points without exit lambdas. That suggests that the missing points have no exit lambda, perhaps meaning they are \"infinite\" or they are the persistent points that belong to the cluster all the way to infinity. In that case, stability would be infinite for those points, which is not useful. So maybe the point_exit_lambdas list actually contains the exit lambdas for ALL points in the cluster, and the size is just the number of points? But then length would equal size. Not the case here.\n\nWait: c2 size 2, point_exit_lambdas length 1. So one point has an exit, one doesn't. Could it be that the exit lambdas are only for points that become noise (i.e., leave the cluster without entering a child)? And points that go to children are not listed because they are accounted in children's exit lambdas? But then for c0, the points that go to c1 and c2 would have exit lambdas equal to child births, but they are not listed. So they would have no exit lambda from c0? Then they would contribute infinite stability? That breaks.\n\nMaybe the point_exit_lambdas represent the lambdas at which points leave the cluster (i.e., either to noise or to children). In HDBSCAN's condensed tree, each point has a \"lambda_last\" which is the lambda at which the point last appears in that cluster (i.e., the maximum lambda for which the point is a member). For points that go to a child, that lambda is the child's birth. For points that become noise, it's the noise lambda. So the point_exit_lambdas should include both. But then why are child births not present? Possibly the point_exit_lambdas are only for \"noise\" points, and the child births are not included because they are implicit from the child's birth. But then for points going to children, the exit lambda from parent is the child's birth, which is known. So we could infer those. The missing points are those that go to children, and we know their exit lambdas because children births are given. So for c0, we have 7 points: 4 go to c1 (exit at 0.5), 2 go to c2 (exit at 0.8), and 1 becomes noise at either 0.4, 0.6, or 0.9? But we have three noise exits listed. So that would be 4+2+3=9 points. So not.\n\nPerhaps the remaining points are those that never leave (persist until infinity). In that case, they have no exit lambda. Then we need to define their exit lambda as some large number or the death of the cluster. But the cluster's death is not given.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, I suspect the correct approach is to compute stability as defined in HDBSCAN: for each cluster, stability = sum_{p in cluster} (lambda_p - lambda_birth), where lambda_p is the lambda at which the point leaves the cluster (i.e., the lambda at which it becomes noise or goes to a child). For points that persist, lambda_p is the lambda at which the cluster itself dies (i.e., the birth of its parent or infinity). But we don't have death lambdas.\n\nMaybe the point_exit_lambdas list is intended to be complete for all points, and the discrepancy in length is just a fixture shape artifact (they didn't ensure length equals size). The task says \"fixture_payload defines input shapes only\" so the actual test cases will have consistent data where length equals size. Therefore, I can assume that point_exit_lambdas length equals size for each cluster. In that case, we simply sum over the list.\n\nLet's adopt that assumption: For each cluster, its points are the elements in point_exit_lambdas. The size is just a count (should match list length). For stability, sum max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda) over all exits.\n\nThen we drop clusters with size < min_cluster_size.\n\nAfter that, we have a list of retained clusters with their stability. Then we need to rank them by decreasing stability, and for ties (same stability), by cluster_id (string) in ascending order? Typically alphabetical. Then we select clusters greedily: for each cluster in ranked order, if no already selected descendant blocks it (i.e., none of its descendants in the hierarchy are already selected), then select it. Then block its ancestors (so they cannot be selected later). The goal is to return \"All retained stability rows and sorted selected IDs.\" The stability rows likely include cluster_id, stability, and maybe other info? The output format is not specified, but presumably a dict with keys like \"stabilities\" and \"selected\".\n\nWe must produce a JSON-serializable object. The function should not mutate case.\n\nImplementation plan:\n\n- Validate input: check that required fields exist (min_cluster_size, clusters, parent). clusters must be list, each with cluster_id, size, birth_lambda, point_exit_lambdas. parent must be dict mapping cluster_id to parent_id or None.\n- Compute dropped: clusters with size < min_cluster_size.\n- For each retained cluster, compute stability = sum(max(0, exit - birth_lambda) for exit in point_exit_lambdas).\n- Build hierarchy: parent dict, children mapping.\n- Determine descendants for each cluster (to know blocking). Since hierarchy is a tree.\n- Sort retained clusters: primary key -stability, secondary key cluster_id (ascending).\n- Greedy selection: initialize selected set empty. blocked set for ancestors? We need to block ancestors of selected clusters. Also must ensure that no selected descendant blocks a new selection. So iterate: for cluster in sorted list, if any of its descendants (including self? \"no already selected descendant blocks it\" means if any descendant (strictly descendant?) is already selected, then this cluster cannot be selected because it would be an ancestor of an already selected cluster. In HDBSCAN selection, you cannot select a cluster if it has a selected descendant. So if any descendant is selected, skip. Then if selected, add this cluster to selected, and mark all its ancestors as blocked (so they cannot be selected later). So we need to traverse ancestors.\n- Also, clusters that are not retained (dropped) are not considered for selection. The parent references may point to dropped clusters? We need to handle that. The parent map includes all clusters, even dropped. The hierarchy is built from the original parent map. When checking descendants/ancestors, we consider all clusters? Or only retained? The selection process typically only considers retained clusters for selection, but blocking should consider the whole hierarchy? Usually, if a cluster is dropped due to size, it is like it doesn't exist. But its ancestors and descendants still exist. The selection algorithm in HDBSCAN works on the condensed tree after pruning small clusters. So we should consider only retained clusters for selection and for blocking relationships. The parent relationships among retained clusters: if a parent is dropped, the child's parent becomes the next retained ancestor? But the problem likely expects to use the given parent map directly. The dropped clusters are removed from the tree. Then the connections between retained clusters are via the original parent relationships but skipping dropped ones. That is more complex.\n\nGiven time, maybe the task expects to treat the parent map as is, and only consider retained clusters for selection and blocking, but ancestors of a selected cluster are all retained ancestors (following parent pointers until None). If a parent is dropped, we skip it and consider the next ancestor that is retained.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the selection is over the original clusters, but we only consider retained clusters for selection. The blocking condition: \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors.\" The descendant relationship is based on the original tree, including dropped clusters? Typically in HDBSCAN, after pruning small clusters, the tree is restructured. But the problem says \"Drop clusters smaller than the minimum cluster size\" and then do stability selection on the remaining clusters. The parent relationships among remaining clusters might be adjusted: the parent of a cluster becomes the nearest ancestor that is not dropped. However, the input parent map includes all clusters. We need to infer the intended behavior.\n\nLet's look at the example: clusters c0 (size7), c1 (size4), c2 (size2), min_cluster_size=3. So c2 is dropped because size 2 < 3. Then the remaining clusters are c0 and c1. c1's parent is c0 (which is retained). The stability of c0 and c1 would be computed. Then selection: choose highest stability. If c1's stability is higher, select c1, then block c0 (its ancestor). If c0's stability is higher, select c0, then c1 cannot be selected because it is descendant of selected c0. That seems straightforward.\n\nBut what if a parent is dropped? E.g., if c0 size 2 (<3), then c0 dropped, but c1 and c2 have parent c0. Then c1 and c2 become root clusters? Their parent is c0 (dropped). In selection, they have no retained ancestors, so blocking is minimal. I think we should treat parent pointer as given, but when checking ancestors, we consider only retained clusters. So if a parent is dropped, it doesn't block. Similarly, descendant checking: only retained descendants matter.\n\nSo algorithm:\n- Parse clusters into dict keyed by cluster_id.\n- Determine which clusters are retained (size >= min_cluster_size).\n- Compute stability for each retained cluster.\n- Build a mapping of children for all clusters (original).\n- Define a function to get retained ancestors of a retained cluster: follow parent pointers up, collect those that are retained (including self? No, ancestors are strictly up). For blocking, we need to block all retained ancestors.\n- Define a function to get retained descendants of a retained cluster: all retained clusters that are descendants in the original tree (including self? Typically descendant means strict descendant for blocking? \"no already selected descendant blocks it\" - if a descendant is already selected, then this cluster cannot be selected. So descendant means strictly lower in hierarchy. So we need to check strict descendants. So we need to collect all retained descendants (excluding self).\n- Sorting: primary key = -stability, secondary = cluster_id (lexicographic).\n- Selection loop: for each cluster in sorted list:\n  - Check if any of its retained descendants is already in selected set. If yes, skip.\n  - Otherwise, add cluster to selected set.\n  - For each of its retained ancestors, add to blocked set? Actually we need to ensure that later clusters that are ancestors of this selected cluster cannot be selected. So we can maintain a set of blocked clusters (ancestors of selected clusters). But the condition \"block its ancestors\" means that after selecting a cluster, any of its ancestors become ineligible for selection. So we add all retained ancestors to blocked set. Then, in iteration, we skip if cluster is in blocked set or if any descendant selected. The descendant check is already covered by the blocked set? Not exactly: an ancestor could be blocked by a descendant, but blocked set will include ancestors. However, we also need to prevent selecting a cluster that has an already selected descendant (even if that descendant is not an ancestor of the candidate). So the condition is: if any selected descendant (strict) exists, then candidate cannot be selected. That is equivalent to: candidate is not in the set of ancestors of any selected cluster (because if candidate is ancestor of a selected cluster, then that selected cluster is a descendant, so candidate is blocked). But if candidate has a selected descendant that is not a direct ancestor? Actually \"has a selected descendant\" means candidate is an ancestor of that selected cluster. So candidate would be in the ancestor set of that selected cluster. So we can maintain a set of all clusters that have a selected descendant (i.e., clusters that are ancestors of selected). But that includes the selected clusters themselves? A selected cluster is also an ancestor of itself? Usually not considered. We'll define \"has a selected descendant\" means strict descendant. So candidate is ancestor of some selected cluster. So we can compute: blocked_ancestors set = union of ancestors of all selected clusters. Then any cluster in that set is blocked. Additionally, we need to ensure that we don't select a cluster that is itself a descendant of a selected cluster? That is covered because if a selected cluster exists, its ancestors are blocked, but its descendants are not blocked (they are lower). However, if we select a cluster, its descendants should not be selected later because they are descendants? Actually the rule \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it\" means that descendant blocks its ancestor, not the other way. So after selecting an ancestor, descendants become ineligible? Wait, the typical HDBSCAN selection: you select clusters bottom-up, so selecting a descendant prevents